### 1.5 Snapshot model — R6Δ (R5-03)

**File records.** Each record is (relpath bytes, kind ∈ {absent, regular, dir}, st_dev, st_ino, size, mtime_ns, ctime_ns, sha256) [P-17 · PDB: include ctime and inode].
- Files are opened with `O_NOFOLLOW` and must resolve inside realpath(`.git`).
- Regular files are capped at 4 MiB.
- A symlink, FIFO, device, oversize or unreadable entry inside the scope is a read failure.
- Directory records make adding or removing an entry visible.

**Scope:**
- `config`, `packed-refs`, `FETCH_HEAD`, and the C04 files;
- everything under `refs/heads/`, `refs/remotes/origin/`, `refs/replace/` and `logs/refs/remotes/origin/`, recursive and sorted.

**Ref record:** T plus the SHA-256 of `for-each-ref --format='%(objectname) %(refname)' refs/heads/ refs/remotes/origin/`. It is taken only after preflight passes.

**Equality** is byte equality of the serialised, sorted record lists, plus ref-record equality when both ref records exist.

**Snapshot completeness (R6Δ).** Every output line carries `snapshot=` with exactly one of these values:

| Value | When | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| `full` | snap0 and snap1 both have file records and ref records, and they compared equal | Equal within the full scope. **Not proof of stability** (see residuals). |
| `files-only` | the initial ref record was never taken (failure in C01–C07, or `snap_refs` raised C10); file records compared equal | Only file records were compared. Ref-level state was **not** compared. |
| `none` | phase A failure (A01–A05) or B01 | No comparison was made. |
| `final-unreadable` | D01 | The final snapshot could not be read. **Stability is not claimed.** |

D02 carries `snapshot=full` or `snapshot=files-only`, naming the scope of the comparison that found the difference.

**Invariants (R6Δ):**
- **I-1.** A provisional K result (any classification) can exist only after the ref record was taken, so every classified line carries `snapshot=full`. If an implementation ever reaches a K result with no initial ref record, it must emit C14 instead.
- **I-2.** A `files-only` comparison may let **only** a provisional C-row result stand: one of C01–C07 or C10. It never lets a classification stand.
- **I-3.** No line with `snapshot` other than `full` may be described as having verified stability.

**Control flow** (§4). It produces these rows:

| Row | Situation | Result |
|---|---|---|
| B01 | Initial file snapshot unreadable | TF `snapshot-unreadable-initial` |
| — | Classification raises an exception | Converted to a provisional C row; the final snapshot is still attempted |
| D01 | Final snapshot unreadable | TF `snapshot-unreadable-final` with the provisional trace |
| D02 | Snapshots differ | TF `snapshot-changed` with the provisional trace; overrides every provisional result |
| D00 | Snapshots equal | The provisional result stands |

**Detects [R]:**
- torn reads: snap1 re-reads every file;
- change-then-restore within scope, via ctime [UR-12].

**Residuals:**
- the object store is not snapshotted;
- anything outside the scope;
- changes within one ctime tick on coarse filesystems;
- privileged clock manipulation;
- transient states that git calls observed and that were restored within one ctime tick.

No atomicity or race-freedom is claimed.

### 1.6 Freshness model — R6Δ (R5-02)

#### 1.6.1 Inputs

All taken from snap0 bytes and stats:
- TR = `logs/refs/remotes/origin/development`;
- OR = every file under `logs/refs/remotes/origin/` (includes TR);
- FETCH_HEAD and its mtime_ns;
- config: `remote.origin.url` (all values), `remote.origin.fetch` (all values), `remote.*.url`, and whether insteadOf / pushInsteadOf is present;
- the current tracking refs (from the ref record);
- `now`.

#### 1.6.2 Reflog grammar

```
^([0-9a-f]{40}) ([0-9a-f]{40}) [^<\n]* <[^>\n]*> ([0-9]+) ([+-][0-9]{4})\t([^\n]*)$
```

- Fields: old, new, ts (UTC epoch seconds), tz (ignored).
- cmdline = the message up to the first `": "`.
- The last line of the file is the newest entry.

#### 1.6.3 R rows (target reflog)

| Row | Condition | Class |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | TR absent or empty | NONE |
| R2 | any line is malformed | CONTRA(malformed) |
| R3 | some earlier line has ts > the last line's ts | CONTRA(non-monotonic) |
| R4 | last line's new ≠ T | CONTRA(newest-not-T) |
| R5 | last line's ts > now | CONTRA(future) |
| R6 | last line's cmdline not accepted | INSUF(newest-unaccepted, v = ts) |
| R7 | otherwise | POS(ts) |

**Accepted cmdlines:** `^(fetch|pull)( |$)`.
- **`update by push`** [P-20 · PDB: **not accepted**, so it falls to R6]. If P-20 is adopted, `update by push` is accepted as well.

#### 1.6.4 F rows (FETCH_HEAD)

**Definitions:**
- **norm(u):** applied in order, each once:
  - (a) strip userinfo (`scheme://user[:pass]@` becomes `scheme://`; scp `user@host:` becomes `host:`);
  - (b) remove one trailing `/`;
  - (c) remove one trailing `.git`.

  Comparison is byte-exact and case-sensitive.
- **Origin lines** have norm(url) = norm(remote.origin.url).
- **Dev lines D** are origin lines of the form `branch 'development'`.
- **Whole-fetch set WF** [P-08 · PDB: as listed here]: `fetch`, `fetch origin`, `fetch --prune`, `fetch --prune origin`, `fetch origin --prune`, `fetch -p`, `fetch -p origin`, `fetch origin -p`.
  - **`--all` forms** [P-12 · PDB: **excluded**]. If P-12 is adopted, they are admitted only when exactly one remote is configured.
  - Any cmdline containing `--append` or a refspec is excluded.

**Evaluation order:**
1. F01–F03 (parse).
2. F04–F05 (admissibility; these stop F evaluation).
3. Corroboration checks P1–P7 (F06–F17), recording the first failure fc.
4. Development decision (F18–F22).

| Row | Check | Failure class / fetch_head |
|---|---|---|
| F01 | FETCH_HEAD absent or empty | NONE / `none` |
| F02 | a line fails `^([0-9a-f]{40})\t(not-for-merge)?\t(branch\|tag\|remote-tracking branch) '([^'\n]*)' of ([^\n]+)$` | CONTRA / `malformed` |
| F03 | mtime > now | CONTRA / `future` |
| F04 | insteadOf or pushInsteadOf present | INSUF / `inadmissible-insteadof` |
| F05 | `remote.origin.url` not single-valued | INSUF / `inadmissible-remote-url` |
| F06 | refspec ≠ exactly `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*` | fc / `uncorroborated-refspec` |
| F07 | any OR file malformed | fc / `uncorroborated-reflog-malformed` |
| F08 | ts* > now | CONTRA / `future` |
| F09 | P1: no OR entry | fc / `uncorroborated-no-fetch-event` |
| F10 | P2: Enew cmdlines differ | fc / `uncorroborated-multiple-fetch-events` |
| F11 | P3: cmdline ∉ WF | fc / `uncorroborated-not-whole-fetch` |
| F12 | P3: an `--all` form with more than one remote (reached only if P-12 is adopted) | fc / `uncorroborated-multi-remote` |
| F13 | P4: a non-origin line is present | fc / `uncorroborated-foreign-lines` |
| F14 | P4: duplicate `branch '<n>'` names | fc / `uncorroborated-duplicate-lines` |
| F15 | P5: content link fails (for each e ∈ Enew with new ≠ 0{40}, the origin line `branch '<name>'` must have sha = e.new) | fc / `uncorroborated-no-link` |
| F16 | P6: completeness fails (excluding development and HEAD, origin branch lines must be in bijection with the current tracking refs, with equal values) | fc / `uncorroborated-incomplete` |
| F17 | P7: \|mtime − ts*\| > W [P-11 · PDB: two-sided; UR-1 · W=120 s provisional] | fc / `uncorroborated-window` (or `uncorroborated-no-reflog` when ts* < mtime − W) |

**Development decision.** "Corroborated" means no fc was recorded.

| Row | Condition | Class / fetch_head |
|---|---|---|
| F18 | corroborated ∧ \|D\| = 1 ∧ D.sha = T ∧ **G-F OPEN** | POS(ts*) / `ok` |
| **F22 R6Δ** | corroborated ∧ \|D\| = 1 ∧ D.sha = T ∧ **G-F CLOSED** | INSUF / `unverified-association` |
| F19 | corroborated ∧ some D.sha ≠ T | NEG / `negative-conflict` [B-12] |
| F20 | corroborated ∧ \|D\| = 0 | NEG / `negative-dev-absent` [P-10 · PDB: NEG] |
| F21 | not corroborated ∧ some D.sha ≠ T | NEG / `negative-conflict-uncorroborated` [P-01 · PDB: NEG] |
| — | not corroborated otherwise | INSUF / the fc row and token |

**What P1–P7 would establish if G-F were OPEN [R; UR-8, UR-10, UR-1].** The association between the development line and the fetch event at ts* holds **only** under these assumptions:
- a whole, non-append origin fetch truncates FETCH_HEAD and writes one line for every fetched branch, unchanged ones included [U13 / UR-10];
- the reflog cmdline strings are as listed [U11 / UR-8];
- W bounds the write gap [UR-1].

These are **unverified**. Matching reflog evidence, tracking refs and FETCH_HEAD content does **not** by itself prove that every line came from one fetch.

**Where mtime is used.** mtime is used only in F17 and only as the file's last-write time. It is never the timestamp of a line and never becomes `observed_at` [B-11].

**Residuals** if G-F is ever opened:
- a refspec-less URL fetch followed by `--no-write-fetch-head` can make observed_at overstate the fetch time by up to W;
- the remote state was observed up to one fetch duration before ts* (this also applies to R);
- the refspec at the time of the fetch is unknown;
- crafted file and reflog pairs are forgery (L-7).

